Schemas for TCSpecial's configuration files: XTCS, a configuration written in
XML, and YTCS, the same configuration written in YAML, and the simulator's own
file in each spelling. Every program that reads a configuration reads one of
these formats, and tcs-gui writes them; the schemas are here, beside
tcsprotocol's XTCE documents, rather than with any one program.

    tcspecial.xsd            XTCS: a configuration, written in XML (.xtcs)
    tcspecial.schema.json    YTCS: the same, written in YAML (.ytcs)
    tcspecial-sim.xsd        a simulator configuration, written in XML
    tcspecial-sim.schema.json  the same, written in YAML, with the
                             world section -- its ambient temperature
                             and its physics model -- which only YAML has

What these are for is the thing an editor cannot do for a file somebody is
writing by hand or with another tool: tell them, in their own editor, that
`payloads` is a section and `payload` is not. They check the *shape* of a file --
which sections there are, what an entry may say, and which words are words --
the node types each section takes among them, and the kinds of payload a
simulator file may simulate.

What they do not check is whether a configuration can be flown. Which attributes
a node of a given type must state, whether two payloads claim one socket,
whether a vote's inputs are the ones its links bind: those are rules about
meaning rather than shape, they are tested against real buses, and `tcs-verify`
is the one place they live. A file these schemas accept may still be refused,
and the refusal will say why. See `docs/tcspecial-design.rst`, "What is checked
before anything runs".

A configuration written by tcs-gui already says which schema describes it, so
an editor opening one needs no setting up: the XML carries
`xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation` on its root element and the YAML carries a
`# yaml-language-server: $schema=` comment at the top. So does the simulator's
file, which names `tcspecial-sim.schema.json` the same way. The path is
relative where the schemas sit above the file, which is a source tree, and the
installed path otherwise. What follows is for a file that does not say, or a
tool that does not look.

A configuration's name ends in `.ytcs`, for YAML, or `.xtcs`, for XML, which
an editor does not know without being told: associate the two with YAML and
XML first. In VS Code, for instance:

    "files.associations": { "*.ytcs": "yaml", "*.xtcs": "xml" }

The simulator's file is `.yaml`, and needs nothing.

A JSON Schema for YAML is not a category error: every YAML document is a JSON
document in another spelling, and JSON Schema is what editors and language
servers actually read. Point yours at the file:

    # .yaml-language-server in a directory of configurations, or a comment
    # at the top of one file:
    # yaml-language-server: $schema=/opt/tcs/share/tcspecial/schema/tcspecial.schema.json

And for the XML spelling, xmllint will check a file against the XSD:

    xmllint --noout --schema /opt/tcs/share/tcspecial/schema/tcspecial.xsd vehicle.xtcs

Installed by `make install-data` under $(PREFIX)/share/tcspecial/schema,
/opt/tcs/share/tcspecial/schema by default.
